WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:04.340
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:09.050
Creating a photorealistic image with fur and
hair is hard.

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:13.870
It is typically done by using light simulation
programs where we use the laws of physics

00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:19.320
to simulate the path of millions and millions
of light rays as they bounce off of different

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:20.699
objects in a scene.

00:00:20.699 --> 00:00:24.289
This typically takes from minutes to hours
if we're lucky.

00:00:24.289 --> 00:00:29.470
However, in the presence of materials like
hair and fur, this problem becomes even more

00:00:29.470 --> 00:00:33.550
difficult, because fur fibers have inner scattering
media.

00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:38.640
This means that we not only have to bounce
these rays off of the surface of objects,

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:43.510
but also have to simulate how light is transmitted
between these inner layers.

00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:48.829
And initially, we start out with a noisy image,
and this noise gets slowly eliminated as we

00:00:48.829 --> 00:00:51.390
compute more and more rays for the simulation.

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:56.410
Spp means samples per pixel, which is the
number of rays we compute for each pixel in

00:00:56.410 --> 00:00:57.410
our image.

00:00:57.410 --> 00:01:03.719
And you can see that with previous techniques,
using 256 samples per pixel leads to a very

00:01:03.719 --> 00:01:09.500
noisy image and we need to spend significantly
more time to obtain a clear, converged image.

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:14.310
And this new technique enables us to get the
most out of our samples, and if we render

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:21.520
an image with 256 spp, we get a roughly equivalent
quality to a previous technique using around

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:23.950
six times as many samples.

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:29.050
If we had a film studio and someone walked
up on us and said that we can render the next

00:01:29.050 --> 00:01:34.270
Guardians of The Galaxy film six times cheaper,
we'd surely be all over it.

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:36.610
This would save us millions of dollars.

00:01:36.610 --> 00:01:42.300
The main selling point is that this work introduces
a multi-scale model for rendering hair and

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:43.300
fur.

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:47.280
This means that it computes near and far-field
scattering separately.

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:53.300
The far-field scattering model contains simplifications,
which means that it's way faster to compute.

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:58.030
This simplification is sufficient if we look
at a model from afar or we look closely at

00:01:58.030 --> 00:02:01.840
a hair model that is way thinner than human
hair strands.

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:07.460
The near-field model is more faithful to reality,
but also more expensive to compute.

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:12.390
And the final, most important puzzle piece
is stitching together the two: whenever we

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:17.500
can get away with it, we should use the far-field
model, and compute the expensive near-field

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:20.860
model only when it makes a difference visually.

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:25.890
And one more thing: as these hamsters get
closer or further away from the camera, we

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:30.130
need to make sure that there is no annoying
jump when we're switching models.

00:02:30.130 --> 00:02:35.440
And as you can see, the animations are buttery
smooth, and when we look at it, we see beautiful

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:40.500
rendered images, and if we didn't know a bit
about the theory, we would have no idea about

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:43.350
the multi-scale wizardry under the hood.

00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:44.350
Excellent work.

00:02:44.350 --> 00:02:49.820
The paper also contains a set of decompositions
for different light paths, for instance, here,

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:53.970
you can see a fully rendered image on the
left, and different combinations of light

00:02:53.970 --> 00:02:56.340
reflection and transmission events.

00:02:56.340 --> 00:03:02.390
For instance, R stands for one light reflection,
TT for two transmission events, and so on.

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:06.070
The S in the superscript denotes light scattering
events.

00:03:06.070 --> 00:03:11.100
Adding up all the possible combinations of
these Ts and Rs, we get the photorealistic

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:12.300
image on the left.

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:14.390
That's really cool, loving it!

00:03:14.390 --> 00:03:18.790
If you would like to learn more about light
simulations, I am holding a full master-level

00:03:18.790 --> 00:03:21.370
course on it at the Technical University of
Vienna.

00:03:21.370 --> 00:03:25.830
And the entirety of this course is available
free of charge for everyone.

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:30.120
I got some feedback from you Fellow Scholars
that you watched it and enjoyed it quite a

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:31.120
bit.

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:32.120
Give it a go!

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:34.569
As always, details are available in the video
description.

00:03:34.569 --> 00:03:38.880
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

